                subject: "Offer the worlds people already have, from every Minecraft folder they own",
                details: "The first step of the wizard used to ask for a path and nothing else,\nwhich is a strange question to put to somebody whose worlds are all\nsitting in one well-known folder. It now finds them.\n\nThe default installation is entry one in a list nobody had to build:\n%APPDATA%\\.minecraft\\saves on Windows, the Library path on macOS,\n~/.minecraft elsewhere, plus a portable .minecraft beside the running\nexecutable when one really exists. More folders can be mounted, because\none machine commonly holds several installs - a vanilla one, a modded\none, a copy on another drive - and each can be labelled, since two\nfolders both called saves tell you nothing apart. Mounting takes the\ninstallation or the saves folder inside it and says which it found.\nUnmounting rewrites one JSON file and never opens the folder; a test\nasserts the worlds are still there afterwards, because \"unmount\" beside\na list of worlds reads as \"delete\" to a reasonable person.\n\nDeliberately no MultiMC or Prism entry. Their instance-root shapes\ncould not be confirmed from anything in this repository, and a guessed\nroot reports \"no worlds\" about a folder full of them - worse than not\nlooking, because it answers a question it never asked. Mounting covers\nthem properly.\n\nEach world shows what people actually choose by, as a real second line:\nthe name from LevelName rather than the folder, when it was last\nplayed, the version, the game mode, hardcore, cheats, its dimensions\nand region files, its size, and which mounted folder it came from -\nbecause two installs commonly hold worlds with the same name. Anything\nunreadable is omitted rather than guessed, and a world whose level.dat\ncannot be read is still listed with everything that was never in doubt.\nThe seed travels as decimal text, since a 64-bit seed does not survive\na JavaScript number.\n\nThe list is a real listbox with one roving tab stop, and focus and\nselection are separate on purpose: choosing a world runs a folder\ninspection, so arrowing down ninety rows must not start ninety of them.\nIts search is the project's own field and builder over the name, the\nfolder, the path, the mount label and every detail part.\n\nTyping a path, browsing for one and dropping a folder all still work,\nnone of them behind a disclosure, all of them working with nothing\nmounted - somebody with one world on a memory stick is a normal user.\nA dropped folder that is already listed resolves to that row instead of\nappearing twice.\n\n118 files, 2258 tests.\n\n---\n\n精靈第一步以前淨係問你要條路,但人哋啲世界全部好地地擺喺一個大家都\n知嘅資料夾入面 — 而家識得自己搵。\n\n預設嗰個安裝係第一行,唔使你加。想加多幾個都得,因為一部機好平常\n裝幾份:原味一份、模組一份、第二個碟仲有一份。每個可以改名,因為\n兩個都叫 saves 嘅資料夾,擺喺埋一齊係認唔出邊個係邊個。\n\n特登冇加 MultiMC 同 Prism。佢哋個 instance 路徑結構,我喺呢個 repo\n入面搵唔到可以confirm嘅嘢,而估錯咗個 root,結果就係對住一個裝滿\n世界嘅資料夾講「冇世界」— 咁衰過唔搵,因為佢答咗一條佢冇問過嘅\n問題。",
