                details: "The handoff had grown into a chronological log written for people who\nwere there: allusive, dense, and dependent on knowing what \"the\npattern\", \"the gate\" and \"D17\" meant before reading. Good as a record,\nuseless as an entry point - and it is read by agents of every size,\nnot only the ones that can afford to reconstruct context from style.\n\nIt now opens with a section written in short declarative sentences\nthat defines every term it uses: what the project is, a glossary\n(PRBM, hires and lowres tiles, the mesher, the oracle, the gate, the\ndecisions, the contracts, the recurring built-tested-unreachable\ndefect), what works today, what does not - including the honest state\nof the first oracle comparison - the exact commands that verify all of\nit, and four numbered steps for whoever picks the work up. The log\nbelow it is unchanged; the summary is the door, not a replacement.\n\n---\n\n本嚟份 handoff 係寫俾「當時在場嘅人」睇嘅：又密又跳,「the gate」\n「D17」呢啲字唔識就成篇睇唔明。做記錄一流,做入口零分 - 而讀佢嘅\nagent 大有大細有細,唔係個個都有本事由文風考古返個 context 出嚟。\n\n而家開頭加咗一節人話:短句、每個術語即場解釋、乜嘢做到乜嘢未做到\n(包括 oracle 第一鋪比較個誠實結果)、驗證命令逐條列、接手四步曲。\n下面本日記一隻字冇郁 - 個摘要係道門,唔係替代品。",
